※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4570

※ A reference is only worth having while what it names is still there.  A name
※ bound further out lasts longer than a place made further in, so the place
※ would be gone -- out of reach and no longer nameable -- while the name holding
※ the reference is still in hand, and the last line here would read a variable
※ the program can no longer name.
※
※ It is the rule a function answering with a reference follows (4562), asked at
※ the third place a reference can escape to: not out of the call and not into a
※ variable at the top level, but outward within one body.  What decides it is
※ how deep the scope each of the two belongs to is, and the reference is walked
※ back to the name whose place it is the way provenance is walked everywhere
※ else.
※
※ Rust asks the same question with the same answer.  C and Odin do not ask it,
※ and a pointer to a block-scoped variable outliving its block is the oldest
※ mistake there is.

let b1 : bool = true
let b2 : bool = true

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    let v1 : mut u32 = 0u32
    let r1 : mut &mut u32 = &v1
    if b1:
        let v2 : mut u32 = 1u32
        if b2:
            r1 ← &v2
    if r1⌖ = 0u32 { 0u6 } else { 1u6 }
